      "writtenOn": "2025-12-10T11:04:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think using param that way here and in other places may be dangerous and I think we should copy them in the first place and use the copy all way long.\n\nWhat I mean in this case:\n1) in line 247 we check `param-\u003edid \u003d\u003d FLPT_DEFAULT_DID`\nlater the host could change the params since it still accessed by host, so lets assume we go to line 259 and sets pgd to host provided one)\n2) in `set_context_entry` (after patch https://android-review.git.corp.google.com/c/kernel/common/+/3818903) the `param-\u003edid \u003d\u003d FLPT_DEFAULT_DID` is checked again but malicious host could change it so it will now use host provided `domain_pgd_gpa` (instead `pkvm_host_ept_pgd`) but `set_context_entry` will skip the domain presence verification and so one.\n\nI think there are other cases which could cause a security issue if the pramas could be changed by the malicious host in the middle. e.g. if params were change later on the  `context_present_cache_flush` could flush wrong bdf and so one?\n\nOr am I missing something?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 236,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 236,
        "endChar": 72
      },
      "revId": "50c2ef7f0352af7e5217e410a9fabd1fac04a009",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6c13964d_c71fab1e",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 73
      },
      "lineNbr": 236,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-10T12:14:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "pv_param is a percpu variable and when host makes a hypercall it disables interrupt and preemption while using this pv_param. So it is guaranteed that the host cpu that invoked this hypercall is not accessing it until hypercall returns and host calls `put_this_pv_param()`.\n\nBut now when I think about it, another cpu might be able to access it if it choses to. So its a security gap I guess. Thanks for bringing this up. Its used in kvm hypercalls as well. @dmaluka@google.com, what do you think?\n\nI can have pkvm verion of get_pv_param and put_pv_param which takes a local pv_param variable and copy from and to to it(similar to kernel\u0027s copy_from_user/copy_to_user). Or probably unmap from host ept while pkvm is using it(but its heavyweight than copy)",
      "parentUuid": "885c4dec_733eb609",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 236,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 236,
        "endChar": 72
      },
      "revId": "50c2ef7f0352af7e5217e410a9fabd1fac04a009",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cf3314c2_bd1c5e13",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 73
      },
      "lineNbr": 236,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-10T13:11:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e another cpu might be able to access it if it choses to.\n\nExactly. So I believe Greg has raised a very good point.\n\nI\u0027ve quickly prepared a fix for the KVM set_msr hypercall: aosp/3884781\n\nThis suggests that this pv_param mechanism is tricky, and it\u0027s a good thing that we are dropping it in v6.18. BTW in v6.18 Chuanxiao has already implemented a convenient interface for passing structures via hypercall arg registers instead of passing them in memory, and allowing to easily increase the number of those registers as needed, and it looks like each of the pvIOMMU hypercall param structures fits in 6 registers, so we\u0027ll probably we able to use that for pvIOMMU without trouble.\n\n\u003e I can have pkvm verion of get_pv_param and put_pv_param which takes a local pv_param variable and copy from and to to it(similar to kernel\u0027s copy_from_user/copy_to_user). \n\nLet\u0027s keep it simple. We can _just_ copy pkvm_lm_context_param to a local variable instead of accessing it by pointer, right?\n\n\u003e Or probably unmap from host ept while pkvm is using it(but its heavyweight than copy)\n\nOr we could have different host EPT page tables for different CPUs. Which is doable (CR3 register is per CPU), but obviously not desirable either (at least because we\u0027d need to reserve N times more memory for the host EPT memory pool).",
      "parentUuid": "6c13964d_c71fab1e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 236,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 236,
        "endChar": 72
      },
      "revId": "50c2ef7f0352af7e5217e410a9fabd1fac04a009",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b11e8e0e_11d22c8c",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pv_iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 73
